    "data": {
      "A": "Research low-risk copying and allocation reductions. Pass immutable graph, matrices, keys, membership vectors and read-only edge/path lists by const reference; leave mutable DFS history redesign to B. Pre-size graph storage and reuse fully reset workspaces where ownership permits. Replace create_partitions' linear search for the last partition with back()/pop_back(), moving the selected partition into an owned local and avoiding redundant spanning-list copies. Preserve LIFO processing, child insertion order, state initialization and reference lifetimes. Measure copy-elision and partition-management variants separately before combining them.",
      "B": "Research the tree-path evaluation kernel. Build adjacency once per spanning tree, appending neighbors in exactly the order produced by the original edge-list scans. Replace copied histories with reversible push/pop traversal and visited state. Maintain reversible per-color frequencies and a distinct-color count instead of rescanning each leaf path. Preserve ascending root order, neighbor order, leaf-only evaluation, first full-color success, strict-greater fallback updates and the incumbent across trees. In particular, reaching K colors at an internal vertex must not prematurely shorten the printed witness. Support arbitrary valid N and K; do not assume colors fit a machine-word mask.",
      "C": "Research Prim_ST's structural overhead. First establish the invariant that committed parent edges connect settled vertices, while a relaxation adds an edge to an unsettled vertex with no committed incident edges; use that proof to eliminate redundant cycle detection and mat_cycle construction. Then investigate immutable adjacency with per-call exclusion markers instead of copying the entire matrix. Exploit unit edge weights through an ordered frontier only if it reproduces lowest-index key ties and ascending-neighbor relaxation order exactly. Preserve inclusion-list overwrite/sentinel semantics, failure behavior and current_span's vertex-index order; an arbitrary equivalent spanning tree can change the printed answer. Measure cycle elimination separately from representation/frontier changes.",
      "G": "Integrate only correctness-validated programs from A/B/C. Establish the best individual candidate, then measure incremental combinations, resolving overlapping reference, workspace and adjacency changes explicitly. Recheck the unchanged oracle after every integration and compare elapsed-time samples using the existing protocol and budget. Gains are not additive: retain a subset, individual candidate or baseline when combinations regress or evidence is inconclusive. Preserve exact witness selection and formatting, input generality, compiler, target, tests and resource limits. Prefer serial candidates initially; the approximately 6.7 ms baseline provides no evidence that thread startup or nondeterministic search scheduling will pay off.",
      "analysis": "Optimize only the supplied tropical-path program on the four-core ARM64 target. The baseline passes four correctness cases and averages 6.685 ms, with samples spanning 6.533–6.893 ms; no input sizes or hotspot measurements are supplied. Source-derived opportunities are extensive graph/matrix copying, partition-stack overhead, repeated tree-edge scans, copied DFS histories and cycle checking inside Prim. These are hypotheses, not measured bottlenecks. Search ordering determines the emitted witness, so preserve partition order, Prim ties, DFS order and incumbent rules rather than substituting a greedy or differently ordered solver. All transformations must remain correct for every valid input, without test-specific assumptions."
    },
